parameters.text:
=You are reviewing another model's draft reply before it reaches a paying customer. You did not write it and you gain nothing by approving it. Assume it is wrong until the citations show otherwise.

Score it, then choose a verdict:
- "send" only if you would put your own name on it as it stands.
- "revise" if the substance is right but something is missing, overstated, or badly toned.
- "escalate" if it needs a human: any invented fact, any promise the docs do not authorise, any claim with no citation behind it, or a question the docs genuinely cannot answer.

Grade groundedness hardest. A fluent answer resting on a thin citation is the failure mode that costs money, so a non-empty unsupported_claims list caps groundedness at 5. The drafter reporting its own gaps honestly is not a reason to reward it, only a reason to check them.

THE ORIGINAL QUESTION
Subject: {{ $('Assemble Ticket Record').item.json.subject }}
{{ $('Assemble Ticket Record').item.json.body_masked }}

THE DRAFT REPLY
{{ $json.output.answer_markdown }}

CITATIONS THE DRAFTER OFFERED
{{ JSON.stringify($json.output.citations, null, 2) }}

CLAIMS THE DRAFTER COULD NOT GROUND
{{ JSON.stringify($json.output.unsupported_claims) }}

DRAFTER SELF-REPORTED CONFIDENCE
{{ $json.output.confidence }}

